# Line endings, decided here rather than by whatever each checkout has
# core.autocrlf set to. Without this a Windows clone rewrites every text file
# on the way in and back out again, so the same content produces a different
# diff depending on whose machine it came from — and the noise arrives in
# files nobody edited.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The migration runner records a sha256 of each file's bytes and refuses to
# run when an applied file's checksum differs (utils/migrations.ts). A checkout
# with core.autocrlf=true would rewrite every line ending and fail that check
# on every file, so the SQL is pinned to LF regardless of platform. Covered by
# the rule above now, and kept anyway: it is the one place where a line ending
# is not a cosmetic matter, and the next person to narrow that rule should
# have to read this.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Binary, said explicitly. Git's own heuristic gets these right today, but a
# NUL-free header is all it takes for it to guess "text" — at which point the
# rule above rewrites bytes inside a font or an image and the file is quietly
# corrupted in the checkout.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.woff2 binary
